[FINAL-TEXT] Root cause confirmed. The iptables rules on router6 are persisted in `/etc/iptables/rules.v4` and drop all forwarded packets arriving on eth2 (the uplink to router3 and the rest of the network).

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The user office on subnets 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 (gateway router6) is completely unable to reach any external destination because of a stateless iptables firewall misconfiguration on router6 that drops all inbound forwarded traffic.

**Affected component:** The FORWARD chain of the iptables firewall on router6, persisted in `/etc/iptables/rules.v4`.

**Mechanism:** router6 serves as the edge router for the 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 office subnets. Its eth2 (10.50.1.2) connects upstream to router3 (10.50.1.1), which has RIP-learned routes to the rest of the network. When pc15–pc18 send packets outbound, they leave through router6 eth2 toward router3 — that direction is unrestricted. However, **return/reply traffic arriving back on eth2 is blocked** by three blanket DROP rules: `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. There is only one narrow ACCEPT exception (TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to a single host). Any ICMP, UDP, or TCP reply from the rest of the network is silently discarded at router6 before it can reach the office PCs.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, matching `-i eth2` (the r6_r3 uplink interface).

**Root cause:** The iptables FORWARD rules on router6 indiscriminately drop all packets arriving from the WAN-side interface eth2, making all external destinations unreachable for office users despite valid routing state.

**Corrective fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from the FORWARD chain (and from `/etc/iptables/rules.v4` to persist the fix): `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`, then update the saved rules file.
=== END ===